oss the  organization  Consultants aren't just useful at  breaking through corporate politics  they're also especially useful when it  comes to making hard unpopular decisions  this is particularly true in the current  economic climate because lots of  unpopular choices need to be made during  downturns for example this might include  shutting down a sector or laying off a  substantial number of employees and a  contrary to popular belief the vast  majority of CEOs don't want to lay off  employees for two reasons one they're  actually good people and they feel bad  cutting off so many people's incomes or  two they're cynical and they don't want  the bad press and reputation associated  with laying off so many people a perfect  example of the latter is Jeff Bezos back  in November on the same day that rumors  started to float around that Amazon was  going to do layoffs Jeff Bezos announced  that he would donate all of his wealth  to charity seems like an odd coincidence  to me but you can be the judge of that  anyway regardless of whether a CEO wants  to avoid layoffs for respectable reasons  or cynical reasons in the end they want  to avoid layoffs but at the same time  they know deep down that it's the right  choice for the business but how are they  going to convince thousands of managers  at tens of thousands of employees and  millions of investors to at least  somewhat agree with them while enter  management Consultants it will  essentially be the consultant's jobs to  justify the layoffs through objective  means for example they might make charts  regarding just how much money a  particular sector is losing and why the  ROI may not be worth it they may also  quantify how much money layoffs could  save the company and how discreet with  the company in a much better position in  case of a deeper recession once McKinsey  or BCG puts together all of this data  the CEO can send out the findings to the  various managers and the directors  across the organization followed by a  heartfelt message they can say something  like I really didn't want it to come  down to this but after seeing this 1 000  page report from McKinsey I feel that  there is no choice but to move forward  with these layoffs this report doesn't  just help the executives either it also  helps the various directors and managers  who will directly be laying off team  members that they've been working with  for years I mean these guys aren't  stupid they also know that making these  Cuts is the right choice but given that  they personally know the people being  laid off it's much harder for them to  pull the trigger they may even fight for  a certain employee or manager but the  McKinsey report does wonders when it  comes to making this process as smooth  as possible with all that being said  though you can bet that if the layoffs  work and the company starts doing well  again the CEO is gonna take all their  credit and say something like you know  it wasn't easy but I was willing to make  the hard choices when no one else wanted  to and that that's why the company is  where it's at today conversely if the  recession pass to buy quickly and the  layoffs turn out to be unnecessary and  they put the company a step behind the  competition the CEO can shift all the  blame to the Consultants they can say  something like you know I never felt  good about the layoffs that's why I  brought in McKinsey and it paid them at  30 million to do a thorough analysis of  the business and the industry and  unfortunately it doesn't look like they  did a good job as you can see management  Consultants are the perfect scapegoat  when it comes to justifying hard choices  and blaming them if the choices don't  quite work out  thank you  in the end objectively speaking  management Consultants are indeed  overpaid that weight one hundred percent  of the time they're gonna be less  experience than you they're gonna know  less about your business and or can I  come to the same conclusions and even if  they don't well it's not like you're  gonna listen to them anyway so  management Consultants rarely ever  provided any tangible value and in an  Ideal World they would be completely  pointless but we don't live in a perfect  world in the real world companies are  filled with politics bureaucracy drama  ego and it leaders want to deflect as  much responsibility as possible and it  turns out that management Consultants  are the perfect scapegoat when it comes  to dealing with all of these corporate  inefficiencies now I know that we've  been trashing on Consultants this entire  video but it's not all bad in fact from  a career perspective it's actually quite  appealing for starters you don't need  any technical skills you don't need to  know C plus plus or Java or the  intricacies of running servers or  networks you simply need to have a  strong communication and people skills  and while this doesn't come naturally to  all of us for most people looking into  Consulting it does aside from not  requiring any hard skills consulting  jobs often come with a lot of perks for  people who like to explore and  experience for example you won't be  doing the same thing over and over again  with each project you'll be working with  a new company traveling to new locations  and Consulting on different issues to  make things even better consulting firms  are often ranked extremely favorably  when it comes to company culture BCG for  example literally ranks number one when  it comes to employee approval of the CEO  Also let's not forget about the  compensation not only are you paid super  well but your entire compensation is in  the form of cash and bonus so for the  better or for the worse you don't have  to deal with any stock compensation with  all that being said though like any job  this job has its cons as well you'll be  working long hours you'll rarely be at  home you'll always be on call and at the  end of your career you'll have little to  quantify please show 